  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n\nCurrently the western half of the CONUS is under southwest flow\nthanks to a large area of low pressure/heights in the Pacific\nNorthwest. The ridge axis is roughly north/south over western\nMinnesota. At the surface a weak low pressure sits in\nsouthwestern North Dakota with a warm front across the South\nDakota border. Lingering storms from a low level jet (LLJ) this\nmorning still continue in far north central ND. These storms\nproduced heavy rain with very little storm motion, so several\nFlood Advisories were issued. The latest one will expire at\n1930z. There are some bubbling cumulus clouds, elevated, in the\nsouth central that have some light radar returns with them.\nAccording to our 18z sounding, there is a heavy capping layer,\nthen plenty of elevated CAPE. \n\nThe rest of today should be mostly quiet storm-wise as the\nsurface low pressure slides to the east, and there are currently\nno shortwave in the southwest flow. Most CAMs have no storms in\nthe area until the LLJ kicks back in after 04z. There is still \na level 1 risk for the eastern 2/3rds of the state for today, \nalthough the next round may not be until after sunset again or\nuntil Sunday. Today will also be very warm with highs in the \nmid 90s, that is roughly 20 degrees above normal. These hot \ntemperatures will continue through Monday, although Monday will \nbe in the 80s. \n\nThe severe thunderstorm risk will continue all holiday weekend \nwith a level 1 risk through Monday as the southwest flow and \nwarm temperatures continue. Sunday the surface low looks to be \nin northeastern South Dakota with an area of 2000-4000 J/kg of \nMUCAPE over North Dakota wrapping around the low. The shear will\nbe marginal with around 15kts in the east and 35kts in the \nwest. Most CAMs do not have much going on until after sunset \nagain when a possible LLJ and kick storms off using that \nelevated MUCAPE. The area of concerned would be around Highway 2\nbetween 6pm and midnight. Sunday night a strong area of \ndiffluence will also be over the state starting around 1am, so \nthere will likely be overnight storms.\n\nLabor Day a Northern Rockies low pressure will swing up to the \nNorthern Plains into eastern Montana. With this, possibly a\nwidespread rain event could occur through the first half of the\nday before North Dakota gets into the dry slot of the low.\nModels have a much smaller area of severe weather, with a skinny\narea of MUCAPE around 2000 J/kg in western ND. Shear will be\nplenty however 40-55kts. A limitation for storms is a strong\nwarm layer at 850mb that will be a capping layer. CAMs otherwise\nhave a line of storms coming out of Montana in the morning off\nthe front.\n\nThe rest of the forecast will be a slow cooling trend with\ncooler air wrapping into the northern Plains. As of now forecast\ntemperatures will reach the 60s and 70s by next Saturday. Next\nweek will also be breezy to windy, especially Tuesday and\nWednesday where an Advisory or High Wind Warning may be needed.",
